[{"ref_id":0,"title":"The low block and the counter-attack: Reading it on air","terms":"The low block and the counter-attack","content":"- A low-block side can have well under 40% possession and still be comfortable.
- Its chances tend to be few but direct: fast breaks, through balls, and runs in behind.
- If it concedes first, it has to come out of the block, which changes the shape of the game."},{"ref_id":1,"title":"Expected goals (xG): What drives it","terms":"Expected goals (xG)","content":"Distance and angle matter most. A shot from close range in front of goal is worth far more than one from 25 metres or from a tight angle near the byline. Match Desk's model uses distance and angle only, so it describes the chance, not the finish."},{"ref_id":2,"title":"The low block and the counter-attack: Counter-attacking","terms":"The low block and the counter-attack","content":"Teams that sit in a low block aim to break quickly when they win the ball, with forwards who run into the space the opponent leaves behind. The danger is in the transition, not in long spells of possession."},{"ref_id":3,"title":"Expected goals (xG): Reading it on air","terms":"Expected goals (xG)","content":"- A single chance above 0.30 is a big chance.
- A team's total xG says how many goals its chances would usually produce. Leading the xG count while trailing on the scoreboard usually means wasteful finishing or a strong goalkeeper.
- Low xG goals (under 0.10) are long-range strikes or awkward angles: rare, and worth highlighting."}]
