[{"ref_id":0,"title":"The low block and the counter-attack: Reading it on air","terms":"The low block and the counter-attack","content":"- A low-block side can have well under 40% possession and still be comfortable.\n- Its chances tend to be few but direct: fast breaks, through balls, and runs in behind.\n- If it concedes first, it has to come out of the block, which changes the shape of the game."},{"ref_id":1,"title":"Physical data: sprints, speed, and distance: Reading it on air","terms":"Physical data: sprints, speed, and distance","content":"Top speeds matter most for forwards running in behind and defenders recovering. A high sprint count late in a match shows a player still pressing or chasing; falling numbers can show fatigue before a substitution."},{"ref_id":2,"title":"Field tilt: Why it matters","terms":"Field tilt","content":"Possession counts every pass, including safe ones in a team's own half. Field tilt only counts the passes that put the opponent under pressure, so it tells you which side is pinning the other back. A team can have less of the ball and still dominate territory, which is common for direct and pressing teams."},{"ref_id":3,"title":"The low block and the counter-attack: Counter-attacking","terms":"The low block and the counter-attack","content":"Teams that sit in a low block aim to break quickly when they win the ball, with forwards who run into the space the opponent leaves behind. The danger is in the transition, not in long spells of possession."}]
