[{"ref_id":0,"title":"Expected threat (xT): How Match Desk uses it","terms":"Expected threat (xT)","content":"Every pass and carry is scored by the threat it adds. A pass that takes the ball from midfield into the space behind a defence adds a lot; a safe pass sideways adds almost nothing. That is how the desk decides which plays were decisive, and it powers the momentum line: each team's share of threat created over the last few minutes."},{"ref_id":1,"title":"Momentum: Momentum shifts","terms":"Momentum","content":"A momentum shift is when the line swings clearly to one side and stays there for a few minutes. It often follows a goal, a substitution, a tactical change, or a spell of pressing, and it often comes before chances."},{"ref_id":2,"title":"The low block and the counter-attack: Counter-attacking","terms":"The low block and the counter-attack","content":"Teams that sit in a low block aim to break quickly when they win the ball, with forwards who run into the space the opponent leaves behind. The danger is in the transition, not in long spells of possession."},{"ref_id":3,"title":"Expected threat (xT): Overview","terms":"Expected threat (xT)","content":"Expected threat values a position on the pitch by how likely possession there leads to a goal in the next few actions. Moving the ball from a low-threat zone to a high-threat one adds threat; losing it gives the threat away."}]
